INT. PRIYA'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

SUPER: "TUESDAY, 11:48 PM"

A narrow room under a slanted ceiling, fairy lights stapled in a sagging U above the bed, a desk buried in chemistry notes, three half-empty mugs, a corkboard crowded with ticket stubs and one photo turned face-down. A laptop glows. Rain ticks at the dormer window while PRIYA IYER, 16, braids and hoodie sleeves chewed to threads, sits cross-legged on the duvet and her older cousin DESMOND OKAFOR, 24, lean, careful, a courier's lanyard still around his neck, stands in the doorway with his car keys pinched between two fingers as the house creaks below them.

INSERT - LAPTOP SCREEN

A cursor blinks inside a locked folder named "DON'T".

BACK TO SCENE

DESMOND
(closing the door behind him with a slow click)
Your mum said you weren't sleeping.

PRIYA
(not looking up, tapping a pen against her knee)
Mum says a lot of things.

DESMOND
(crossing to the desk, lifting a mug, sniffing it, setting it down)
This is tea from Sunday.

PRIYA
(shrugging one shoulder)
It's a collection.

This is the last ordinary minute either of them will have.

DESMOND
(sitting on the desk chair, turning it to face her)
You texted me at two a.m. Just a street name. Harrow Lane.

PRIYA
(pulling the laptop an inch closer to her stomach)
Wrong number.

DESMOND
(a small, patient smile)
Priya. It was my number.

POV - DESMOND

The corkboard. The face-down photo. A corner of it lifting in the draft from the vent.

PRIYA
(following his gaze, standing too fast, pressing the photo flat with her palm)
Don't.

DESMOND
(hands open, palms up, leaning back)
I haven't moved.

PRIYA
(sitting again, farther from the board)
You were on Harrow Lane that night. Weren't you.

DESMOND
(rubbing his thumb along the lanyard)
I'm on lots of lanes. It's the job.

PRIYA
(opening the laptop, turning it so he can see)
Eleven deliveries. Nine of them are the same house.

INSERT - LAPTOP SCREEN

A spreadsheet. Dates in a column. A single address repeated. A timestamp, 11:52 PM, highlighted yellow.

DESMOND
(his eyes on the screen, his jaw working once)
Where did you get this.

PRIYA
(very quietly)
Your app. You left yourself logged in on the family iPad.

DESMOND
(standing, walking to the window, pulling the curtain a finger's width)
You went through my account.

PRIYA
(not moving)
A girl went missing from that house, Des.

DESMOND
(without turning, his reflection fogging the glass)
Girls go missing from lots of houses.

PRIYA
(opening a drawer, taking out a small silver key and holding it up)
She dropped this in your bag. Mum found it in the car in March and gave it to me because I "like puzzles."

DESMOND
(turning, eyes on the key, his voice level)
Put that down.

PRIYA
(closing her fist around it)
It opens the locked folder. Doesn't it? The one you made for her.

DESMOND
(a step forward, then stopping himself)
Give me the key and I'll explain it.

PRIYA
(shaking her head, standing on the bed so she is taller than him)
Explain it first.

SMASH CUT TO:

INT. PRIYA'S BEDROOM - CONTINUOUS

The rain is louder now. The lights above the bed flicker once. Downstairs a television is switched off and the house goes quiet in a way that feels listened-to.

DESMOND
(lunging a half-step, catching himself on the desk, knocking a mug to the floor where it does not break)
You think I did something to her.

PRIYA
(holding the key behind her back)
I think you were there at 11:52.

DESMOND
(pointing at the screen, his voice cracking upward)
I was there because she called me! She called ME. She asked for a ride, and I was forty minutes away, and I told her to wait inside, and she didn't, and I have driven that lane every night since, because if I had been faster--

PRIYA
(frozen, the key slipping in her sweaty fingers)
You never said.

DESMOND
(sinking onto the chair, hands over his face)
Nobody asked me the right question.

INSERT - THE FACE-DOWN PHOTO

Pressed against the cork. A girl's handwriting on the back: "for Des. don't be late."

PRIYA
(peeling the photo off the board, turning it over, her breath leaving her)
She wrote this to you.

DESMOND
(lifting his head, wet-eyed, looking at the key in her hand)
Open the folder, Priya. Please. I never could.

PRIYA
(kneeling at the laptop, sliding the key into a small drive port, her hand shaking)
If it's something awful--

DESMOND
(a very small nod)
Then it's hers. And we'll know.

ON SCREEN

The folder opens. A single video file. The thumbnail shows a dark street and a figure in a yellow raincoat looking back over her shoulder.

PRIYA
(her finger over the trackpad, not pressing)
Together?

DESMOND
(moving to sit beside her on the bed, close, not touching)
Together.

PRIYA
(pressing play, the sound already rising from the speakers like a held breath)
Okay.

SLOW PUSH IN on the screen as the rain outside the window finds the same rhythm as the rain in the video, and neither of them blinks.

CUT TO BLACK.
